    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We define the following relation between the domain and range of $f$:\n\\begin{align*}\ny=f(x) &= 3x-8\\\\\nx=f^{-1}(y) &= \\frac{y + 8}{3}\n\\end{align*}The statements within each parenthesis will only be true if the arithmetic that occurs inside them are solely operated on by the same functions. Therefore, we may assert that:\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= 3x-8\\\\\nx &= \\frac{y + 8}{3}\n\\end{align*}We can set up the following equation:\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= 3x-8\\\\\nx &= \\frac{y + 8}{3}\\\\\n3x &= y + 8\\\\\nx &= \\frac{y+8}{3}\n\\end{align*}According to our question, we must have that $y=x$, which will result in a solution.\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= 3x-8\\\\\nx(x)=\\frac{x+8}{3}\\\\\n3x^{2}-9x+8 &= 0\\\\\n(3x-4)(x-2) &= 0\\\\\nx &= 2~~\\text{or}~~x = \\boxed{\\frac{4}{3}}\n\\end{align*}. The answer is \\frac{4}{3}",
